  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 121 AM EDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nThe pattern for the week ahead can be summarized as being situated \nbetween ridging aloft to the south and west, and troughing across \nthe north with continuous shortwaves rolling in from the NW. This \nplaces us within a general NW flow pattern. No real airmass change \nor noticeable frontal passage in sight, as the area is forecast to \nrecord similar temperatures each day this week. Humidity will remain \nelevated and mostly unchanged, with 70s dew points (in the valley) \nexpected. However, ridging is forecast to shift over us next \nweekend, providing the possibility of hotter temperatures and drier \nweather.\n\nLater today has trended drier with recent/newer runs of the CAMs. \nActivity remains well north of the area, and then across the \nsoutheast, where an isolated shower/storm could develop this \nafternoon mainly south of I-40.\n\nPossibly during the afternoon hours Tuesday, will need monitoring, \nas a pronounced shortwave will cross the Great Lakes and Ohio Valley. \nAn MCS or remnants of one, will approach the area from the north \naround mid-day Tuesday. 00z runs of CAMs mostly show different \nsolutions and general uncertainty. Day 3 SPC issued yesterday \nafternoon does bring a slight risk of severe weather for northern \nportions of the CWA.\n\nGiven the general pattern we'll be in for the coming week, paired \nwith shortwave impulse approaches under unstable conditions, showers \nand thunderstorms will be in the forecast each day in some capacity. \nThis time of year does favor increased chances of MCS occurrence, but \nthe further out in time, the greater the uncertainty as far as \ntiming, extent, and severity. The best chance of storm activity will \nbe during the PM hours, with gusty winds and flash flooding the main \nthreats.",
